Here is a sample rubric for evaluating a software engineer's work, in JSON format with Markdown ticks:

```json
{
  "criteria": [
    {
      "name": "Code Quality",
      "description": "Is the code well-organized, readable, and maintainable?",
      "levels": [
        {
          "level": "Exceptional",
          "description": "Code is extremely well-organized, readable, and maintainable. It is clear that the engineer has taken the time to refactor and optimize the code.",
          "points": 5
        },
        {
          "level": "Good",
          "description": "Code is well-organized, readable, and maintainable. Some minor improvements could be made, but overall the code is of high quality.",
          "points": 4
        },
        {
          "level": "Fair",
          "description": "Code is somewhat disorganized, and readability could be improved. Some major improvements are needed to make the code maintainable.",
          "points": 3
        },
        {
          "level": "Needs Improvement",
          "description": "Code is poorly organized, and readability is a major issue. Significant improvements are needed to make the code maintainable.",
          "points": 2
        },
        {
          "level": "Unacceptable",
          "description": "Code is extremely poorly organized, and readability is almost impossible. The code is not maintainable in its current state.",
          "points": 1
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "Functionality",
      "description": "Does the code meet the requirements and work as expected?",
      "levels": [
        {
          "level": "Exceptional",
          "description": "Code meets all requirements and works flawlessly. The engineer has clearly tested the code thoroughly.",
          "points": 5
        },
        {
          "level": "Good",
          "description": "Code meets most requirements and works well. Some minor issues may exist, but overall the code is functional.",
          "points": 4
        },
        {
          "level": "Fair",
          "description": "Code meets some requirements, but has significant issues. The engineer may not have thoroughly tested the code.",
          "points": 3
        },
        {
          "level": "Needs Improvement",
          "description": "Code does not meet most requirements, and has major issues. The engineer needs to revisit the code and make significant improvements.",
          "points": 2
        },
        {
          "level": "Unacceptable",
          "description": "Code does not meet any requirements, and is completely non-functional.",
          "points": 1
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "name": "Testing",
      "description": "Are there sufficient tests to ensure the code works as expected?",
      "levels": [
        {
          "level": "Exceptional",
          "description": "Tests are comprehensive, well-written, and cover all scenarios. The engineer has clearly prioritized testing.",
          "points": 5
        },
        {
          "level": "Good",
          "description": "Tests are good, but could be improved. Some scenarios may not be covered, but overall the tests are sufficient.",
          "points": 4
        },
        {
          "level": "Fair",
          "description": "Tests are minimal, and do not cover all scenarios. The engineer may not have prioritized testing.",
          "points": 3
        },
        {
          "level": "Needs Improvement",
          "description": "Tests are lacking, and do not provide sufficient coverage. The engineer needs to revisit the tests and make significant improvements.",
          "points": 2
        },
        {
          "level": "Unacceptable",
          "description": "No tests exist, or they are completely inadequate.",
          "points": 1
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}
```

This rubric assesses the software engineer's work based on three criteria: Code Quality, Functionality, and Testing. Each criterion has five levels of achievement, ranging from "Exceptional" to "Unacceptable". The points assigned to each level can be used to calculate an overall score.

